DFS in Python: Recursive
def dfs_recursive(graph, start, goal):
    parent = {start: None}
    expanded = []

    def visit(state):
        expanded.append(state)
        if state == goal:
            return True
        for next_state in graph[state]:
            if next_state not in parent:
                parent[next_state] = state
                if visit(next_state):
                    return True
        return False

    found = visit(start)
    path = path_from(parent, goal) if found else None
    return path, expanded

This version uses the listed neighbor order directly because each recursive call begins before the loop considers the next neighbor. It returns True to stop all active calls once it finds the goal. Without that return propagation, the search would continue after finding a solution.

